    "title": "When is Easter 2026, and why does the date change year to year?",
    "text": "Four years into the Russia-Ukraine war, one Anacortes woman is fighting back with fundraising, humanitarian trips and a classroom full of pen pals. ANACORTES, Wash. — Four years into Russia's invasion of Ukraine, an Anacortes mother is doing everything she can to make sure the war doesn't fade from memory — or from conscience. Marketa Vorel founded the Sunflower Fund after the invasion began in February 2022. Since then, she has raised more than $800,000 that goes directly to the Ukrainian people in the form of funding for a women and children's clinic, war crime investigations and military aid. On Monday, Vorel visited Island View Elementary in Anacortes, where fourth and fifth graders wrote letters and drew pictures for Ukrainian schoolchildren as part of a pen pal project. \"You do what you can,\" Vorel said. \"It is certainly alleviating the feeling of hopelessness in a world of uncertainty.\" The war has claimed as many as 140,000 Ukrainian lives, with countless others wounded in what has become a grinding, seemingly endless conflict. For students at Island View, the lessons are difficult ones to comprehend. \"It's like, while we're having a good life over here, and they have to be on their toes all the time in case their house gets bombed,\" said student Roz Guzik. The pen pal project takes on added meaning for some families in Anacortes. A squadron from nearby Naval Air Station Whidbey Island is currently deployed to the conflict in the Iran, meaning some of these children are navigating their own fears about loved ones in harm's way. \"Their parents might be in the military and they could pass away in the war,\" said student Jamie Bratten. \"So it's pretty sad.\" Vorel, who grew up in Czechoslovakia during the Cold War, said she knows firsthand what Russian oppression feels like — and that experience drives her work. \"It's my hope that this generation will continue this connection so that in a world that feels really uncertain and dangerous, we also have some hope for peace,\" she said. Vorel plans to return to Ukraine in March to continue her humanitarian work.",
